<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  JaBot entitlements — Developer ID + Hardened Runtime, not Mac App Store.

  This file is the audit, not just the grant list. Every entitlement JaBot
  could plausibly want is accounted for below: the one it genuinely needs is
  declared, and the ones it does not need are named with the reason they are
  absent. Adding a key here widens what a compromised adapter subprocess can
  reach, so a key without a justification is a bug.

  Hardened Runtime is required for notarization and is enabled in
  tauri.conf.json (`bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime`). App Sandbox is
  deliberately off: a sandboxed app cannot exec `claude` / `codex` / a custom
  harness from the user's PATH, cannot open arbitrary registered folders as
  ACP `cwd`, and cannot supervise grandchild process groups. JaBot is a
  process supervisor; the sandbox is the wrong box, and that is also why the
  Mac App Store is not the channel (docs/research/app-shell/process-architecture.md).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Subprocess spawn. THE one JaBot cannot ship without.

	  Every thread runs an ACP adapter the host execs from the user's PATH:
	  npm-installed Node CLIs, Homebrew binaries, a shell shim, whatever a
	  Tier-3 custom harness JSON points at. None of that carries our Team ID.
	  Under Hardened Runtime, library validation refuses code not signed by
	  the same team or by Apple; for an app in this category the first symptom
	  is the adapter dying at startup with `code signature invalid` in a build
	  that works perfectly unsigned, which is exactly the failure the shell
	  research warned would cost a day.

	  This weakens us to code injection via loadable libraries. Accepted:
	  JaBot's whole purpose is running third-party agent binaries the user
	  chose, so the trust boundary is the harness catalog and the permission
	  broker (#20), not dyld.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Audited and deliberately NOT declared:

	  com.apple.security.network.client / .server
	      Sandbox-only keys. Outgoing HTTPS to harness APIs and to the update
	      feed already works for a non-sandboxed Developer ID app; declaring
	      them here would be cargo cult. They become required the day anyone
	      proposes the App Store — which is the day to re-read the note above.

	  keychain-access-groups
	      Only needed to SHARE keychain items with another app of ours. The
	      secrets vault (#9) reads and writes items JaBot itself created under
	      its own access group, which needs no entitlement.

	  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit / .allow-unsigned-executable-memory
	      WKWebView evaluates JavaScript in a system-provided WebContent XPC
	      process with its own entitlements, not inside JaBot.

	  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
	      The host sets env vars FOR adapter children (e.g.
	      HERMES_ACP_SKIP_CONFIGURED_MCP=1). It never needs DYLD_* injected
	      into itself.

	  com.apple.security.cs.debugger / get-task-allow
	      get-task-allow must be absent or notarization rejects the build.
	      Debug builds are unsigned and never go through this path.

	  Camera, microphone, location, address book, calendars, photos
	      JaBot touches none of them. Adding an unused device entitlement
	      still shows up in the Gatekeeper consent prompts users see.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
